! jb_af1c "PAU GATES ROUTINE", the SHFR1 and SHFR2 blocks, p.180 and
! p.199 (octal 022400.40-022424.40).
!
! WHY THESE TWO NEXT.  SHF0, the block before them, turned out to be
! nearly blind on the model: its shift is by zero, so its expected
! values ARE its input tables, and because the compares index them
! through the SAME register as the operands, a wrong index moves both
! sides together.  Ignoring the index register entirely, or running
! the loop once instead of twice, both leave it reporting no error.
!
! I EXPECTED SHFR1 AND SHFR2 TO FIX THAT, AND THEY DO NOT.  The
! reasoning was that their expected values come from SEPARATE tables --
! WRDAR1/WRDBR1 and WRDAR2/WRDBR2 -- so the compares would no longer
! move with the operands.  Measured by injection, that is wrong: with
! the 24-bit VFL effective address ignoring its index register
! entirely, these blocks STILL report no error and still reach their
! exit.  The separateness of the table is irrelevant.  What blinds the
! block is that both sides -- operands AND expected values -- are
! indexed through the SAME register, so a wrong index selects entry 0
! on both sides and entry 0 matches entry 0.
!
! The corrected rule: a block that indexes its operands and its
! expected values through one register cannot detect a wrong index, no
! matter how many tables it has.  Only an assertion on state outside
! its own compares can see it, which is why the suite entry checks the
! accumulator's final contents.
!
! A SECOND MEASUREMENT WORTH KEEPING.  Making CBR never take its
! branch, so each block runs one iteration instead of two, leaves
! EVERYTHING passing here -- including the accumulator check that
! caught the same injection on SHF0.  The reason is that CBR+ adds one
! to the value field whether or not it branches: SHFR1's single pass
! uses entry 0 and still advances X1, so SHFR2's single pass uses
! entry 1 and the final accumulator is the value two full loops would
! have left.  Two blocks running once each are indistinguishable, at
! the end, from one block running twice.
!
! WHAT THEY DO.  Each iteration loads WRDA[i] into the accumulator's
! left word (64 bits at offset 64 = IBM 0-63) and adds WRDB[i] into
! the right (offset 0 = IBM 64-127), then shifts the 96-bit fraction
! -- IBM 12-107 -- RIGHT by one place (SHFR1) or two (SHFR2), and
! compares IBM 12-63 against WRDAR*'s bits 12-63 and IBM 64-107
! against WRDBR*'s bits 0-43.
!
! THE ARITHMETIC, computed from the tables before the first run.  The
! two table entries give 96-bit fractions of alternating bits:
!
!   iteration 0   frac96 = aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa
!   iteration 1   frac96 = 555555555555555555555555
!
! so a right shift of one turns the first into 5555... and the second
! into 2aaa..., and a right shift of two turns them into 2aaa... and
! 1555... .  All four land exactly on the WRDAR*/WRDBR* entries.  The
! tables are built from alternating bits precisely so that a shift of
! n is visible as a different constant for every n.
!
! ERROR EXITS ARE INLINE HERE, unlike SHF0's branch to the shared
! CHKSZ: PE4A/PE4B and PE6A/PE6B sit in the block with their own
! SIC,SEN and B,SERS, and the listing names each failure -- "R1
! SHIFT-HI 52 BITS OR EXP DEC", "R1 SHIFT-LOW 44 BITS", and the same
! pair for R2.  So this program needs no framework substitution beyond
! SERS and SEN.
!
! HOW THE SHIFT COUNT IS SPELLED.  The listing writes SHF(U),-1 and
! SHF(U),-2; this assembler takes the ENCODED operand instead, exactly
! as the existing F212 programs write SHFL(N),128.0 for a shift of one.
! The encoding is AMOUNT x 128, plus 64 when the shift is to the right,
! which three independent rows of this routine confirm:
!
!     SHF(U), 0   -> 000000.47      SHF(U), 1  -> 000200.47   (128)
!     SHF(U),-1   -> 000300.47      SHF(U), 2  -> 000400.47   (256)
!     SHF(U),-2   -> 000500.47      SHF(U), 3  -> 000600.47   (384)
!     SHF(U),-3   -> 000700.47
!
! so 192 is right-one and 320 is right-two.  Written as the encoded
! value rather than guessed from the octal: the pattern was derived
! from the pairs above and then checked against every row.
!
! ONE WORD BEFORE THE BLOCK: in the routine, X1 arrives already
! refilled by the previous block's CBR+, which refills from XA when
! the count reaches zero.  Standing alone, this program does its own
! LX at 022400.00 -- the half-word the listing gives to the previous
! block's B,SSW -- so every address from 022400.40 matches the column.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0
SENO    DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- index control word and data tables (p.199) --
        SLC, (8)24117.0
XA      XW, 0, 2, XA
WRDA    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000125252525252525252
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000052525252525252525
WRDB    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1252525252525250000000
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0525252525252524000000
WRDAR1  DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000052525252525252525
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000025252525252525252
WRDBR1  DD(BU,64,8), (8)0525252525252524000000
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)1252525252525250000000
WRDAR2  DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000025252525252525252
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000012525252525252525
WRDBR2  DD(BU,64,8), (8)1252525252525250000000
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0525252525252524000000

        SLC, (8)22400.0
SAP25   LX, $X1, XA
! -- SHIFT RIGHT ONE --
SHFR1   L(BU,64), WRDA($X1), 64
        +(BU,64), WRDB($X1), 0
        SHF(U), 192.0                        ! -1: right one, see the note below
        CT0110(BU,52), WRDAR1+.12($X1), 64
        BZB, $RZ, PE4A
        B, $+1.32
PE4A    SIC, SEN
        B, SERS                              ! R1 shift-hi 52 bits or exp dec
        CT0110(BU,44), WRDBR1($X1), 20
        BZB, $RZ, PE4B
        B, $+1.32
PE4B    SIC, SEN
        B, SERS                              ! R1 shift-low 44 bits
        CBR+, $X1, SHFR1
        B, $+1.
        B, SHFR1
        SIC, SENO+.32
! The listing has B,SSW here, the SSIP return between blocks.  Standing
! alone this program runs the two blocks back to back, so that row is
! SUBSTITUTED by a branch to the next half-word -- the same kind of
! substitution as SERS and SEN, and the only row in this program whose
! encoding is not checked against the listing.
        B, $+.32
! -- SHIFT RIGHT TWO --
SHFR2   L(BU,64), WRDA($X1), 64
        +(BU,64), WRDB($X1), 0
        SHF(U), 320.0                        ! -2: right two
        CT0110(BU,52), WRDAR2+.12($X1), 64
        BZB, $RZ, PE6A
        B, $+1.32
PE6A    SIC, SEN
        B, SERS                              ! R2 shift-hi 52 bits or exp dec
        CT0110(BU,44), WRDBR2($X1), 20
        BZB, $RZ, PE6B
        B, $+1.32
PE6B    SIC, SEN
        B, SERS                              ! R2 shift-low 44 bits
        CBR+, $X1, SHFR2
        B, $+1.
        B, SHFR2
        SIC, SENO+.32
PASS    BEW, $                               ! substitutes for B, SSW
        END, SAP25
